  Tailing the log is a small server route plus a reader on the report page. The file is already NDJSON.\
  trace.jsonl is one JSON object per line, appended as the run goes.\
\
  What that file contains today is sparse. A line is written for a finished stack (type: "finding", with\
  stack_id and determination), an early exit, a kill_after, or an error. Stage changes go to run.json when a\
  stage flushes, not onto this log. Token and tool-call text never land here, because each model call waits\
  for generateText to finish before anything is recorded.\
\
  Server. Add GET /api/runs/:id/trace on the existing Node server. It should:\
\
  \'95 Reject a run id that escapes runs/.\
  \'95 Send content-type: application/x-ndjson and flush headers immediately.\
  \'95 Write the lines already in trace.jsonl, then keep reading new bytes from the last offset.\
    createReadStream stops at the current end of file, so the follow has to be a short offset loop or\
    fs.watchFile. Hold back a trailing partial line until the next newline.\
  \'95 If the file does not exist yet, wait briefly and start once the run creates it.\
  \'95 Stop when the client disconnects, or when run.json status is completed, failed, resumed_completed, or\
    partial.\
  \'95 Close the response after that. Do not buffer the whole run and send it at the end.\
\
  Page. On the report route, fetch that URL, read response.body with a reader, split on newlines, and\
  JSON.parse each complete line. Append those rows above the report. Keep the existing JSON poll for the\
  finished ImpactReport; the stream is the activity list, and the poll still loads the ranked report when the\
  run ends. Abort the reader when the page unmounts or the run id changes.\
\
  That shows stacks finishing, in order, while the review is in flight. Stage labels and live model text\
  would each be a separate addition: one event() call next to each stage flush, or switching the model loop\
  from generateText to streamText and logging those deltas.\
